group: Td
formula: Ni(CO)4
construction: Fragment orbitals (extended Hückel)
headline: 
note: Fragment levels from extended Hückel (qualitative) · energies in Hartree · labels in T<sub>d</sub> · 7 levels hidden (Show all levels)
about: **Where the fragment levels come from.** The left and right columns are the orbitals of the metal atom and of the ligands on their own, from a quick extended Hückel calculation. Each is placed at the energy it has inside the molecule before it mixes with anything. This puts every bonding level below the two levels it comes from and every antibonding level above them, as in a textbook diagram. Treat their positions as qualitative.\n\n**How reliable each fragment level is: 24–100 %.** A fragment level can only be placed using the orbitals the calculation reported. The percentage says how much of the fragment orbital those orbitals cover. Levels below 90 % show their percentage next to them; their positions are less certain.\n\n**Lines.** A line joins a molecular orbital to a fragment orbital that makes up at least 10 % of it. Both members of a bonding/antibonding pair are joined to both of their parents.\n\n**Symmetry labels** are in T<sub>d</sub>, the symmetry of the Ni and its four donor atoms. A ligand's own atoms can lower the symmetry of the whole molecule (the hydrogens of NH3, for example), so the labels may be higher than the molecule's own.\n\n**Not shown:** 47 molecular orbitals that are less than half made of the fragments' valence orbitals. Nearly all of them are high, empty orbitals. 7 further levels sit on the ligands alone or have little metal character; tick Show all levels to draw them. Also not shown: 17 core orbitals below −2.50 Hartree.\n\n**Total electrons: 50** (Ni d10, ligands 40).\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in Td; the code reported none.
